{
  "id": "the-mask-matters",
  "name": "The Mask Matters",
  "category": "beginner",
  "difficulty": 2,
  "xp": 200,
  "level": 2,
  "description": "One PC is cut off, and every address looks correct.",
  "brief": "PC-01 and PC-03 talk to each other happily. PC-02 is on the same switch, has an address that starts with 192.168.1, and yet it can reach nobody.\n\nThe subnet mask decides which addresses a host believes are local. Look at all three PCs and work out why PC-02 disagrees with the others about where it lives.",
  "hints": [
    "Run ipconfig on each PC and compare the three lines carefully.",
    "255.255.255.192 splits a /24 into blocks of 64 addresses.",
    "With that mask, is .200 in the same block as .10?",
    "Give PC-02 the same mask as its neighbours, and an address in range."
  ],
